"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[77],{700077:e=>{e.exports="# Data Type Mapping\n\nSource dialect type → DBSQL type mapping. Includes precision notes and behavioral differences.\n\n## MSSQL\n\n### Types with behavioral differences\n\n| T-SQL                 | DBSQL             | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| --------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `BIT`                 | `BOOLEAN`         | `0`/`1` → `false`/`true`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `MONEY`               | `DECIMAL(19,4)`   | No native MONEY type                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| `FLOAT`               | `DOUBLE`          | T-SQL FLOAT is 8-byte                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| `REAL`                | `FLOAT`           | T-SQL REAL is 4-byte                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| `DATETIME`            | `TIMESTAMP`       | DATETIME has 3ms resolution; TIMESTAMP has microsecond                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `DATETIME2`           | `TIMESTAMP`       | Databricks has microsecond, not nanosecond                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| `DATETIMEOFFSET`      | `TIMESTAMP`       | **Timezone offset is lost**: `DATETIMEOFFSET` stores the UTC offset as part of the value (e.g. `2024-03-20 14:00:00 +05:30`). DBSQL `TIMESTAMP` has no per-value offset — it stores a point in time and timezone display is a session-level setting. If the source relied on per-row offsets, decide before migration: normalize all values to UTC on write (`CONVERT(datetime, col AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')` in T-SQL before export), or add a separate `STRING` column to preserve the original offset string. |\n| `SMALLDATETIME`       | `TIMESTAMP`       | 1-minute resolution; upconvert                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| `TIME`                | `STRING`          | No native TIME type; store as `HH:MM:SS` format. Use `CAST(time_col AS TIMESTAMP)` for arithmetic or `DATE_FORMAT` for display.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `UNIQUEIDENTIFIER`    | `STRING`          | Store as 36-char string; use `UUID()` to generate                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `XML`                 | `STRING`          | Parse with XPath/xpath_string functions. For JSON / semi-structured patterns, use `VARIANT` type or `parse_json()` to extract fields.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| `NVARCHAR(n)`         | `STRING`          | Databricks uses UTF-8 natively                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| `VARCHAR(MAX)`        | `STRING`          | No length limit; see notes below                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `NVARCHAR(MAX)`       | `STRING`          | No length limit; DBSQL is UTF-8 natively so no separate Unicode handling needed                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `NTEXT` / `TEXT`      | `STRING`          | Deprecated in T-SQL; always STRING                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `IMAGE` / `VARBINARY` | `BINARY`          |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `HIERARCHYID`         | `STRING`          | No native equivalent; store as string path                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| `GEOGRAPHY`           | `GEOGRAPHY(4326)` | Native type available (DBSQL + DBR 17.1+, preview). DBSQL `GEOGRAPHY` only supports SRID 4326 (WGS84) — matches T-SQL `GEOGRAPHY` which also uses WGS84. Not supported on Iceberg tables. Fallback for older runtimes: store as `STRING` in WKT format and use `st_geogfromtext()` to read. Use `ST_*` functions (e.g. `ST_DISTANCE`, `ST_WITHIN`) for spatial operations.                                                                                                                                  |\n| `GEOMETRY`            | `GEOMETRY(srid)`  | Native type available (DBSQL + DBR 17.1+, preview). Supports ~11,000 SRID values. Preserves T-SQL planar geometry semantics. Not supported on Iceberg tables. Fallback for older runtimes: store as `STRING` in WKT format and use `st_geomfromtext()` to read. Use `ST_*` functions (e.g. `ST_DISTANCE`, `ST_WITHIN`) for spatial operations.                                                                                                                                                              |\n\n### Identical types (no translation)\n\n`INT`, `BIGINT`, `SMALLINT`, `DECIMAL(p,s)`, `NUMERIC(p,s)`, `DATE`\n\n### VARCHAR(MAX) / NVARCHAR(MAX) notes\n\n- **Two-tier storage removed**: T-SQL stores `VARCHAR(MAX)` values ≤ 8,000 bytes inline in the row, then overflows to LOB storage (up to 2 GB). DBSQL `STRING` has no such storage split — no migration action needed, but be aware that read/write performance characteristics may differ for large values.\n- **Index/key restrictions lifted**: T-SQL prohibits `VARCHAR(MAX)` as a primary key, index key, or partition key. DBSQL `STRING` has no such restrictions — this is a safe improvement.\n- **NVARCHAR vs VARCHAR**: Both map to `STRING`. DBSQL is UTF-8 natively, so the narrow/Unicode distinction disappears in migration. No separate handling for `NVARCHAR(MAX)` vs `VARCHAR(MAX)`.\n- **COLLATION**: T-SQL `VARCHAR(MAX)` columns carry a collation (often CI_AS — case-insensitive). DBSQL `STRING` is case-sensitive by default. Add `COLLATE UTF8_LCASE` on the column or comparison if case-insensitive matching is required (see `references/tsql/00-quirks.md Case sensitivity`).\n\n### TINYINT — signed/unsigned drift\n\nT-SQL `TINYINT` is **unsigned** (0–255). DBSQL `TINYINT` is **signed** (−128–127). Values 128–255 stored in a T-SQL `TINYINT` column will silently overflow or error when written to a DBSQL `TINYINT`. Use `SMALLINT` as the safe migration target unless column values are guaranteed to stay within 0–127.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL (stores 200 fine)\ncol TINYINT\n\n-- DBSQL — overflow risk if values exceed 127\ncol TINYINT    -- risky\n\n-- DBSQL — safe drop-in replacement\ncol SMALLINT\n```\n\n### BIT → BOOLEAN\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL uses 0/1\nINSERT INTO t (is_active) VALUES (1);\nSELECT * FROM t WHERE is_active = 1;\n\n-- DBSQL uses true/false\nINSERT INTO t (is_active) VALUES (true);\nSELECT * FROM t WHERE is_active = true;\n\n-- Migration cast: CASE WHEN bit_column = 1 THEN true ELSE false END\n```\n\n### Decimal precision\n\nDatabricks arithmetic on DECIMAL can produce different results due to precision promotion rules:\n\n```sql\n-- Division increases precision; if combined precision exceeds 38, rounding occurs\n-- Workaround: cast to smaller precision before operations\nSELECT CAST(a AS DECIMAL(18,4)) / CAST(b AS DECIMAL(18,4))\n```\n\n### Conversion functions\n\n| T-SQL                     | DBSQL                    | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ------------------------- | ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CONVERT(type, expr)`     | `CAST(expr AS type)`     | Direct replacement in most cases                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| `TRY_CONVERT(type, expr)` | `TRY_CAST(expr AS type)` | **Preserve VARCHAR(N)**: `TRY_CONVERT(VARCHAR(20), col)` → `TRY_CAST(col AS VARCHAR(20))`, NOT `TRY_CAST(col AS STRING)`. T-SQL `TRY_CONVERT` to `VARCHAR(N)` truncates strings longer than N chars. Databricks `TRY_CAST` to `VARCHAR(N)` applies a length constraint (exact behavior for overlong strings — truncation vs NULL — is runtime-dependent); `TRY_CAST(col AS STRING)` applies no length constraint at all and always passes the full value through. |\n| `PARSE(expr AS type)`     | `CAST(expr AS type)`     | No locale-aware parsing in DBSQL; use `CAST`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n\n**TRY_CAST overuse — match the source intent**: Use `TRY_CAST` only when the source uses `TRY_CONVERT` / `TRY_CAST`, or when it has explicit NULL-on-failure handling (e.g. `ISNULL(TRY_CONVERT(...), default)`). Do **not** substitute plain `CAST` or `CONVERT` with `TRY_CAST` — this silently changes behavior: a conversion error that would abort the query in T-SQL becomes a NULL in DBSQL, hiding data quality issues.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL (plain CAST — errors on bad data)\nSELECT CAST(amount AS DECIMAL(10,2)) FROM orders\n\n-- DBSQL (CORRECT — preserve error behavior)\nSELECT CAST(amount AS DECIMAL(10,2)) FROM orders\n\n-- DBSQL (WRONG — silently returns NULL on bad data, masking errors)\nSELECT TRY_CAST(amount AS DECIMAL(10,2)) FROM orders\n```\n\n> For CONVERT date formatting style codes (CONVERT style 101, 112, 120, etc.), see `references/tsql/10-functions-date.md`.\n\n## SYNAPSE\n\nSame as MSSQL plus:\n\n| Synapse          | DBSQL    | Notes                                |\n| ---------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| `NVARCHAR(4000)` | `STRING` | Synapse max VARCHAR/NVARCHAR is 4000 |\n"}}]);
